;; RING-STAGED tile gradients — numeric proof (endeavor 138 rings under --differentiate).
;;
;; WHY THIS SPEC EXISTS.  All six 138 specs were skipped with "ring/pipelining is forward-only;
;; tile AD is a later chapter", which was true when written.  That chapter is now.  But 138's
;; own specs cannot settle whether ring gradients are CORRECT: 03/04/05 are sm_90a PTX kernels,
;; and VERIFY-AUTODIFF has only :l0 and :opencl runtimes, so no NVIDIA backward can be driven
;; numerically at all.  Un-skipping them on "it compiles" would repeat the 135/01 mistake — that
;; one compiled, had the right atomics, and returned exactly 0.0.
;;
;; So the ring MECHANISM is proven here, on hardware we can actually run, and 138/01-03 are then
;; un-skipped on the strength of THIS rather than on their own compilation.
;;
;; WHAT WAS WRONG.  `ring-get` is a pure view selector, and endeavor 138 taught the FORWARD path
;; to treat a compound tensor target as a first-class tile.  The AD path was never told, and
;; assumed a tile argument was a SYMBOL:
;;
;;     The value (CRISP.COMPILER:RING-GET TILES 0) is not of type SYMBOL
;;
;; — a raw Lisp type error rather than a Crisp diagnostic.  The rule that fixes it is the
;; general one: THE ADJOINT OF A VIEW IS THE SAME VIEW OF THE ADJOINT.  `(ring-get R i)` has
;; adjoint `(ring-get R_ADJ i)`, so the existing %load-tile-at-bwd / %store-tile-at-bwd edges
;; apply unchanged.  A ring is rank+1 scratch; its adjoint is a ring of the same shape.
;;
;; SCOPE, deliberately narrow.  This exercises a ring slot as a STAGING TARGET — the case the
;; fix covers — and nothing else.  Indexing a slot directly, `(~ (ring-get R i) row col)`, is
;; still not differentiable: the tilde backward wants a symbol target, and RING-GET is not
;; gradient-inert (a view selector is not a no-op for the chain rule, it RE-TARGETS it).  That is
;; a separate rule and is not claimed here.
;;
;; THE CHECK.  The kernel stages A through a ring slot and writes it out, so C = A and
;; d(sum C)/dA[1,0] = 1.0 exactly.  Chosen because it isolates the two ring edges — the load's
;; scatter and the store's gather — with no arithmetic in between to mask a wrong one.
;;
;; SKIP-WITH[--debug]: "pre-existing --debug DIBuilder fault (plan/bugs.md #033) — a hard SBCL memory fault at address 5, not a Crisp diagnostic.  13 of the 19 specs in this directory already carry this skip; 18 was simply missed.  VERIFIED PRE-EXISTING rather than assumed: reproduced identically with src/ and overlays/ checked out at the 145 merge (5a6eebd) and rebuilt, so it predates endeavour 146 entirely.  Of the four 145 specs lacking the skip (14, 15, 17, 18) only this one faults, so the trigger is the scratch RING under --debug specifically."
;; COMPILE-WITH[--ir-target=spv --differentiate]: PASS
;; VERIFY-AUTODIFF: A=4x4@0:0.01 at.A=1,0 h=0.5 atol=1e-3 expect.A=1.0 output-mat=4x4

(def-type mat-t (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))

(def-kernel ring_staged_copy (A &out C)
  (declare #'(mat-t &out mat-t)
           (global-size :set-to 1)
           (local-size  :set-to 1))
  (let ((tiles (make-scratch-matrix-ring float (4 4) :ring-count 2)))
    ;; Slot 1, not slot 0: a wrong slot index in the backward would still pass if the adjoint
    ;; collapsed to the ring's base, so the non-zero slot is what makes the view rule testable.
    (load-tile-at  A (ring-get tiles 1) (0 0))
    (store-tile-at (ring-get tiles 1) C (0 0))))
